Paper:Prompting Techniques for Secure Code Generation: A Systematic Investigation.

Background & Motivation

LLMs (GPT-3/3.5/4) are increasingly used for code generation, but prior studies show they frequently produce insecure code containing common CWEs (e.g., CWE-78, CWE-259, CWE-330).

Developers tend to trust LLMs excessively, often overlooking security flaws in generated code.

Although many prompting techniques exist, their impact on secure code generation has been largely unexplored.


Research Gap

Existing work on code security focuses on evaluating LLM outputs—not on understanding how prompt design affects vulnerability rates.

Prior code-generation studies mostly use zero-shot or few-shot prompts; no systematic comparison of broader prompting strategies exists.

What This Paper Contributes

Conducts the first systematic literature review (SLR) identifying 15 prompting techniques potentially applicable to code generation across 5 categories:

Root (zero-shot, few-shot)

Refinement-based (RCI, self-refine, progressive hint)

Decomposition-based (least-to-most, self-planning)

Reasoning-based (CoT, zero-shot CoT, self-consistency)

Priming-based (persona, memetic proxy)

Adapts these techniques to secure code generation for the first time.


Methodology

Selects 5 non-demonstrative techniques for deeper evaluation: zero-shot, zero-shot CoT, RCI, persona/memetic-proxy, progressive hint.

Uses 150 security-relevant NL prompts from LLMSecEval (covering 18 CWE categories).

Generates Python (and later C) code with GPT-3, GPT-3.5, and GPT-4.

Assesses code validity and security using Bandit and validates with CodeQL.


Key Findings

Prompting style has a significant measurable impact on code security.

Recursive Criticism and Improvement (RCI) consistently reduces security weaknesses the most—particularly on GPT-4.

Zero-shot CoT and persona prompts also show moderate improvements.

Some techniques (e.g., progressive hint) are unstable or impractical for complex coding tasks.


Overall Insight

Prompt engineering is not only a usability strategy—it is an effective security intervention.

Structured prompting can meaningfully decrease vulnerabilities in LLM-generated code, indicating a strong need to integrate prompt-based security practices in real-world LLM coding workflows.
